# API Environment Variables
#
# Copy this file to apps/api/.env and fill in the blanks.
#
# NestJS reads .env from the process working directory, and PM2 starts the API
# with cwd ./apps/api (see ecosystem.config.js) — so the file must live at
# apps/api/.env, not at the repo root.
#
# Everything the API actually reads is listed below. Variables with a value
# shown are safe defaults; variables left blank are either required or switch a
# feature off while empty.

## Required Variables

### Database
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://your-mongodb-connection-string

### Authentication
JWT_SECRET=your-super-secret-jwt-key-minimum-32-characters
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=your-refresh-token-secret-key-minimum-32-characters
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=7d

### Application
NODE_ENV=development  # Set to "production" in production
# main.ts falls back to 3001 when unset. PM2 runs the API on 5000 in
# production (ecosystem.config.js) with Nginx proxying /api to it.
PORT=3005

### Sign in with Google ("Continue with Google")
# Leave both blank and nothing breaks: the Google strategy is not registered,
# /auth/providers reports google:false, and the button does not render. Fill
# them in and the button appears on the login and sign-up pages — no OTP, no
# password to invent.
#
# Where to get them — https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   1. Create an OAuth client ID, application type "Web application".
#   2. Under "Authorised redirect URIs" add EXACTLY the GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL
#      below. Google rejects anything that differs by even a trailing slash.
#        https://propertydealer.pk/api/auth/google/callback
#        http://localhost:3005/api/auth/google/callback      (local only)
#   3. Under "Authorised JavaScript origins" add the site's own origin:
#        https://propertydealer.pk
#        http://localhost:3000                               (local only)
#      Origins are the page the button is clicked from, never the callback,
#      and they carry no path — Google rejects a path here.
#   4. On the OAuth consent screen, the only scopes needed are email + profile.
#
# Note the port in the production URL: there isn't one. The browser only ever
# talks to https://propertydealer.pk, and Next's rewrite in next.config.ts
# forwards /api/* to the API on 127.0.0.1:5000. Putting :5000 or :3010 into
# either box sends Google to a port the public internet cannot reach.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Must include the /api prefix — the whole API is mounted under it.
#   local:      http://localhost:3005/api/auth/google/callback
#   production: https://propertydealer.pk/api/auth/google/callback
GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:3005/api/auth/google/callback

### Storage
STORAGE_DISK=local  # Options: local, s3
#
# "local" writes to <repo>/uploads and serves it at /uploads/... — this is what
# the live site uses today.
#
# "s3" covers S3, Cloudflare R2 and MinIO. These are the names the code reads
# (packages/storage/storage.service.ts); AWS_* names are NOT read.
# STORAGE_BUCKET=your-bucket-name
# STORAGE_KEY=your-access-key-id
# STORAGE_SECRET=your-secret-access-key
# STORAGE_REGION=auto                 # "auto" for R2, e.g. us-east-1 for S3
# STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com   # R2 / MinIO
# STORAGE_PUBLIC_URL=https://img.propertydealer.pk   # optional custom domain
#
# ⚠️ Switching disks changes the URLs of newly uploaded images. Files already
# on disk keep their /uploads/... URLs, so existing pages and their rankings
# are unaffected — but do not move or rename what is already live.

### Frontend URL (for CORS)
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
# VERCEL_URL is also accepted as an allowed origin when present. Vercel sets it
# itself for preview deployments — you never set this by hand.

### Canonical host (SEO)
# Used to build absolute URLs in sitemaps, blog/page/property SEO output and
# IndexNow pings. No protocol, no trailing slash.
APP_HOST=propertydealer.pk
# Absolute public URL of the website. Also where the Google callback sends
# people once they are signed in (WEB_URL is checked first, then this, then
# FRONTEND_URL).
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

### IndexNow (optional)
# Key shared with Bing / Yandex so new and updated pages are pushed for
# crawling. Must match the key file served by the web app and
# NEXT_PUBLIC_INDEXNOW_KEY. Leave blank to switch the pings off.
INDEXNOW_KEY=

### Listing analytics
# Views, impressions and contact taps are summed in memory and written as one
# bulk update every few milliseconds-worth of traffic, so a popular listing is
# not a write hot-spot. Lower = fresher numbers on the agent's Insights page,
# more database writes. A restart loses at most this much counting.
COUNTER_FLUSH_MS=3000

### Redis cache (recommended)
# Connection URL for the local / managed Redis. Default below is for a
# Redis instance running on the same VPS without password.
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379
# Set to "false" to fully bypass the cache (useful for debugging).
REDIS_ENABLED=true

### ISR / Web revalidation webhook
# Public URL of the Next.js front-end so the API can call its
# /api/revalidate endpoint after admin writes (property approve, blog
# publish, settings update, etc.). HTTPS in production.
WEB_URL=http://localhost:3000
# Long random string shared with the web app. The web app rejects
# /api/revalidate calls without this exact secret in the
# `x-revalidate-secret` header.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
REVALIDATE_SECRET=

### Media library — image processing
# Every new upload is converted to WebP. The quality is high on purpose: WebP
# at 92 is visually indistinguishable from the original for photographs, and
# the saving comes from the format being ~30% smaller than JPEG, not from
# discarding detail. Raise to 95 for near-lossless; do not go below 85.
IMAGE_WEBP_QUALITY=92
# Longest edge of the stored image. Images are never upscaled.
IMAGE_MAX_EDGE=2000
# Width of the thumbnail the media grid loads.
IMAGE_THUMB_WIDTH=480

### Image SEO — AI alt text (OpenAI vision)
# With this set, every uploaded image gets alt text, a title and a caption
# written automatically, using the surrounding context (property title, blog
# title, material + city). Without it, uploads still work — the alt text is
# derived from the file name instead, and the "Write with AI" button is
# disabled in the media library.
#
# gpt-4o-mini is the right model here: images are sent downscaled to 768px at
# "low" detail, so a description costs a fraction of a cent.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# Set to "false" to switch the AI off without removing the key.
IMAGE_AI_ENABLED=true
# Safety valve on the bill — descriptions per calendar day.
IMAGE_AI_DAILY_LIMIT=2000

### Material rate consolidation
# The seven per-material collections (doorrates, woodrates, sandrates,
# tilerates, bajrirates, steelrates, bricksrates) duplicate the unified
# `materialrates` schema exactly, plus a `materialType` field.
#
# Leave this UNSET (or "false") and everything behaves as it always has:
# each /api/<material>-rate endpoint reads its own collection.
#
# To consolidate:
#   1. npm run migrate:material-rates --workspace=apps/api            (dry run)
#   2. npm run migrate:material-rates --workspace=apps/api -- --apply (copies data)
#   3. Set MATERIAL_RATES_UNIFIED=true and restart the API.
#   4. Verify every /today-<material>-rate-in-pakistan page, then retire the
#      seven per-material modules.
#
# The migration copies rather than moves, so rolling back is: unset this flag.
MATERIAL_RATES_UNIFIED=false

### Listing API (for n8n / external automation)
# A long random string (>= 32 chars). Required to enable the /api/listing-api/* endpoints.
# Generate with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
LISTING_API_KEY=

# (Optional) ObjectId of the user that should own listings created via the API.
# If not set, the first ADMIN user in the database is used.
LISTING_API_OWNER_ID=

## Production Deployment

When deploying to Vercel/Railway, make sure to set all these environment variables in your deployment platform dashboard.

**Critical for production:**
- `NODE_ENV=production` - Required for secure cookies
- `MONGODB_URI` - Your production MongoDB connection string
- `APP_HOST` / `APP_URL` / `WEB_URL` - The real domain, or SEO output and the
  Google redirect will point at localhost
- `GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL` - Must be the https URL, and must be registered in the
  Google Cloud console
- All JWT secrets should be strong, random strings

### Admin alerts → Discord
# Every event an admin needs to know about (a listing held for approval, a new
# account, a package purchased, a report) is pushed to a Discord channel as an
# embed with a link straight to the thing. The admin's phone then does the
# notification — no Firebase keys, no APNs certificates, no device tokens.
#
# Where to get it: Discord → your server → Server Settings → Integrations →
# Webhooks → New Webhook → pick the channel → Copy Webhook URL.
#
# Leave it blank and alerts are simply off; nothing else changes.
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

### Listing brain (spam / fraud screening)
# Scores every new agent listing. Clean listings publish themselves; anything
# suspicious goes to the approval queue WITH the reasons attached, which the
# dashboard shows on the row.
#
# Set to "false" to go back to "every agent listing waits for approval".
LISTING_BRAIN_ENABLED=true
# Score (0-100) at or above which a human checks it first. 45 is strict on
# purpose: one phone number in the description is enough on its own, because
# that is the behaviour this site most needs to stop. Raise it to be more
# permissive, lower it to hold more.
LISTING_BRAIN_THRESHOLD=45
# The AI second opinion reuses OPENAI_API_KEY (see above). It can only ever
# make a verdict stricter, never looser, and with no key the rules still run.
LISTING_BRAIN_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
